<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.1.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for the signed .app. Each one is a capability the
  app is granted, so each needs a reason to be here.

  Removed: com.apple.security.cs.debugger. That entitlement grants task_for_pid —
  the right to attach a debugger to *other* processes on the machine. It is for
  debugging tools, not for an app, and nothing here ever used it. Verified by
  running embed-rs (ONNX inference + UMAP, the heaviest native path we ship) under
  the hardened runtime without it: completes normally.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <!--
      V8 JIT. Chromium cannot run without it.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      Also V8: it maps writable-executable pages that are not themselves signed.
      Part of electron-builder's default entitlement set for the same reason.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      Load-bearing today, and the failure mode if it is wrong is total: dyld refuses
      to map Electron Framework and the app dies before showing a window.

      Measured, rather than assumed. Signing the packaged bundle with the hardened
      runtime and *without* this entitlement produces:

        dyld: Library not loaded: @rpath/Electron Framework.framework/Electron Framework
          Reason: … not valid for use in process: mapping process and mapped file
          (non-platform) have different Team IDs

      That test used an ad-hoc signature, which has no Team ID at all, so it may be
      stricter than a real Developer ID build where every nested binary shares one
      team. The evidence that it *could* go: nothing third-party is dynamically
      loaded — `ort` is statically linked (no load-dynamic, no download-binaries, and
      `otool -L` on transcribe-rs/embed-rs shows only /usr/lib and /System/Library),
      electron-builder's `files` list excludes node_modules so no native .node ships,
      and the only dylibs in the bundle are Electron's own libffmpeg and
      libvk_swiftshader.

      To actually remove it: build with a real Developer ID
      (`npm run package:mac`, no notarization needed), delete this key, and launch
      the resulting .app. If it opens a window, the entitlement was unnecessary.
      That needs the signing cert, so it could not be settled here.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
